Spec: IOPMP v0.8.1 conflated execute and read source permissions under SPS; v0.8.2 adds dedicated SRCMD_X/SRCMD_XH registers so execute authority is independently revocable per RRID
  [OK] drivers/clk/thead/clk-th1520-ap.c:1250-1261  th1520_vo_gate_clks (via static CCU_GATE definitions)
      These are six CCU_GATE clock gate macro invocations defining clock enable/disable controls for IOPMP hardware block clock signals (iopmp_vosys_dpu_pclk, iopmp_vosys_dpu1_pclk, iopmp_vosys_gpu_pclk, iopmp_dpu1_aclk, iopmp_dpu_aclk, iopmp_gpu_aclk). They toggle clock signals via register bit writes (0x0, bits 23-29); they do not read or write any IOPMP permission registers (SRCMD_R, SRCMD_X, SRCMD_RH, SRCMD_XH, iopmpcfg). No SPS configuration, no permission bitvector programming, no execute/read distinction logic. The CLK_IS_CRITICAL flag on the ACLK gates (lines 1257, 1259, 1261) prevents the kernel from ever gating those clocks, but this is normal for bus-clock infrastructure. No security-relevant code exists here.
  [OK] include/dt-bindings/clock/thead,th1520-clk-ap.h:121-126  N/A (header file, preprocessor constants)
