PR #209  overall: potential_risk
Spec: IOPMP v0.8.1 conflated execute and read source permissions under SPS; v0.8.2 adds dedicated SRCMD_X/SRCMD_XH registers so execute authority is independently revocable per RRID
  [OK] include/sbi/sbi_domain.h:22-48  (header definitions)
      READ, WRITE, EXECUTE are defined as independent bits (1<<0, 1<<1, 1<<2 for M-mode; 1<<3, 1<<4, 1<<5 for SU-mode). SBI_DOMAIN_READ/WRITE/EXECUTE enum (lines 24-26) are also independent bits. This is the correct architectural foundation: EXECUTE is never implied by READ. Any IOPMP driver consuming these flags has the bit layout needed for independent SRCMD_X vs SRCMD_R programming.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:220-271  sbi_domain_check_addr
      Independently tests READ (lines 236-238), WRITE (239-240), and EXECUTE (242-243) against region flags. Returns true only if ALL requested bits are present (line 266: (rrwx & rwx) == rwx). READ access never implies EXECUTE access — each is independently verified. This is the correct canonical pattern that any IOPMP analog (sbi_iopmp_check_src_access) must replicate.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:124-144  sbi_domain_get_oldpmp_flags
      Maps SU_READABLE→PMP_R (line 136-137) and SU_EXECUTABLE→PMP_X (line 140-141) as independent, non-overlapping translations. READ does not imply EXECUTE and vice versa. This is the architectural precedent for how domain flags translate to hardware permission bits. An IOPMP analog (sbi_domain_get_iopmp_srcmd_flags) must independently map EXECUTABLE→SRCMD_X rather than conflating with SRCMD_R.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:146-218  sbi_domain_get_smepmp_flags
      The shared-read-only region (SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_IS_SUR_MR, line 163-164) maps to (PMP_L | PMP_R | PMP_W | PMP_X) — PMP_X is set even though the domain flags have no EXECUTE bits. This is correct for Smepmp encoding semantics (where the encoding {L,R,W,X}={1,1,1,1} means 'shared read-only'), but it is dangerous as a template: if a naive IOPMP developer copies this pattern, they would set SRCMD_X whenever dealing with a 'shared' or 'read-only' region, exactly reproducing the old-spec execute-via-read conflation. The invariant 'Smepmp encoding != IOPMP SRCMD encoding' is not enforced anywhere.
  [RISK] docs/domain_support.md:4-8  (documentation)
      The only explicit mention of IOPMP in the entire codebase — line 7 lists it alongside PMP/ePMP/SiFive Shield as a platform feature for domain isolation. The documentation does NOT describe how IOPMP source-level permissions (SRCMD) map to domain memory region flags, does NOT warn about the old-spec execute-via-read conflation, and does NOT specify that an IOPMP driver must independently test EXECUTE against SRCMD_X. This documentation gap leaves platform integrators without guidance — the most likely failure mode is assuming SRCMD_R suffices for instruction-fetch gating.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.h:16-39  (struct sbi_hart_protection)
      Defines the sole registration interface for memory protection mechanisms. An IOPMP driver would register a struct sbi_hart_protection with configure()/unconfigure() callbacks. The vulnerability surfaces at this interface: the configure() callback receives no explicit guidance about independent SRCMD_X programming. The interface itself is neutral, but there is no validation, no documentation contract, and no type-system enforcement that would prevent an IOPMP driver from using SRCMD_R to gate instruction-fetch. The invariant 'configure() must independently program execute bits' is entirely implicit.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.c:52-62  sbi_hart_protection_configure
      Dispatches to the best-rated protection mechanism's configure() callback. The dispatch logic itself is correct: it selects the highest-rated mechanism and delegates. The risk is entirely downstream in whatever IOPMP driver implemention is registered. The code here does not (and cannot) validate that the driver correctly separates SRCMD_X from SRCMD_R.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-169  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Core domain context switch: unconfigures protection at line 127, switches domain assignment (lines 116-124), then reconfigures protection at line 128. If an IOPMP driver is registered, these two calls reprogram IOPMP SRCMD entries on every domain switch — a chokepoint where the old-spec conflation bug would be deployed on every context transition. There is no invariant check between unconfigure and configure that would detect or prevent the bug. The SATP mode switch at line 139 includes an SFENCE.VMA (line 140), showing awareness of TLB coherency, but there is no analogous IOPMP synchronization (e.g., a fictitious 'iopmp_fence' or SRCMD barrier).
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_init.c:380-404  init_coldboot
      Cold boot path: calls sbi_domain_startup() then sbi_hart_protection_configure() at line 394. This is the initial IOPMP configuration point where SRCMD entries for all sources would be first programmed. Also invoked at warm startup (line 473) and warm resume (line 494) — all three paths invoke an IOPMP driver's configure(). Each is a deployment point for the read/execute conflation bug. The initialization order is correct (domains before protection), but there is no assertion, self-test, or verification that the programmed SRCMD entries correctly separate execute from read. The invariant 'at this point all IOPMP SRCMD bits are correctly programmed' is assumed but never verified.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_domain.h:141-160  (convenience macros)
      SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_READABLE (line 141-143) = SU_READABLE | M_RWX. The M_RWX component includes M_EXECUTABLE, so the macro simultaneously grants M-mode execute even when only 'readable' was intended. SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_EXECUTABLE (147-149) has the identical expansion. The macros mask the read/execute distinction: a naive IOPMP driver that maps MEMREGION_READABLE → SRCMD_R (and neglects to check the EXECUTABLE bit independently) would grant execute to any source that has read access, because the macro itself conflates them for M-mode.Wrote 3 report(s) → output/riscv-iopmp/pr-209/

  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_domain.h:56-83  (shared region macros)
      SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_SHARED_RDONLY = SU_READABLE | M_READABLE (lines 56-58) — correctly contains no execute bits. Under IOPMP SPS, this MUST map to SRCMD_R=1, SRCMD_X=0. An old-spec IOPMP driver gating instruction-fetch via SRCMD_R would make this 'read-only' region executable for DMA sources — a direct manifestation of the vulnerability pattern. The flag definition is correct, but there is no guard preventing an IOPMP driver from misinterpreting it. The IS_SUR_MR macro (lines 81-83) only checks for the RDONLY pattern against ACCESS_MASK; an IOPMP driver using this macro might assume it describes a complete permission set.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:307-340  sbi_hsm_hart_start
      Checks SBI_DOMAIN_EXECUTE independently at line 323-324 before starting a HART. The call passes ONLY SBI_DOMAIN_EXECUTE (not SBI_DOMAIN_READ | SBI_DOMAIN_EXECUTE), so the check independently verifies execute permission without any assumption that read implies execute. This is the correct usage pattern for execute permission in the codebase. For IOPMP, DMA initiators would need analogous independent EXECUTE checks — a source's SRCMD_X must be separately verified from SRCMD_R.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:121-218  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      The concrete PMP driver that iterates domain memory regions and translates flags to hardware entries via sbi_domain_get_smepmp_flags() (line 176 and 202). Line 166-174 track firmware PMP entries to preserve them across domain switches — demonstrating awareness of entry lifecycle. But line 202 calls sbi_domain_get_smepmp_flags() which encodes PMP_X for shared-read-only regions (SUR_MR). If this pattern is mindlessly copied to an IOPMP driver, the IOPMP configure() would set SRCMD_X whenever processing shared regions, reproducing the old-spec conflation. The code is correct for Smepmp but serves as a dangerous architectural template.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain.c:497-526  sbi_domain_check_addr_range
      Range-based permission check that iterates over address sub-ranges and delegates to sbi_domain_check_addr() (line 513), which correctly separates READ/EXECUTE. The function itself is correct. However, its existence highlights that for IOPMP, NO equivalent range-check exists for DMA/bus-initiator accesses — there is no sbi_iopmp_check_src_access() or sbi_iopmp_check_src_range(). Bus-initiator accesses to memory are not gated by domain permissions at all in the current codebase. The codebase provides per-HART protection (PMP/Smepmp) but has zero per-source (IOPMP) access-control infrastructure.

